00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:50.400
You can also raise your hand
on Zoom and I'll call you in.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.230
And we also have
a Slido link which

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:56.550
we'll share again where you can
ask questions anonymously too.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:00.270
But something I was really
excited to start off

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:04.080
with asking is, David, I know
you think a lot about building

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:07.890
great user interfaces
and I was curious to hear

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.280
tips you have for all of us on
communicating with the business

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.020
through data visualizations.

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:17.306
Yeah, sure.

00:08:17.306 --> 00:08:22.490
So I think we started a
big project last year,

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:25.570
which is around
probability of success.

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.480
And then for this
project, we really

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:32.720
thought about another way to
start our senior projects,

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:36.919
especially from the user
interface perspective.

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:40.150
So I would say, when
I joined Novartis

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:42.220
at the beginning,
most of the time,

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:46.150
we were rushing into
code, we developed

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:47.950
the interface, the app.

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:50.180
And then we showed the
app to the end user

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:53.320
and then they are not
happy with the result.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.200
We completely
changed the process.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:00.130
And with this project, we
basically start with wireframe.

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:03.370
So we've met with the user.

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.770
We draw the user requirements.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:08.770
We discussed together
for one week, two weeks.

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:13.120
And then we present some
mockups, some wireframing.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.330
So you could basically
use a paper and a pen

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:19.570
or you can use more
sophisticated tools.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:21.790
I would say it's
a bit of a pity,

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:26.140
but most of the good wireframing
tools you have to pay.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:29.800
We are using
Balsamiq at Novartis.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:31.360
You may use it, you may not.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:33.640
It depends if you know.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:36.220
And then based on
that, we spend around

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.310
I would say, one month before
we can come to an agreement.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:46.070
And then we can start to code.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:49.200
So we really developed the
application of the end user.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.780
That's just from
our perspective.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:53.915
So it really satisfies
what they want.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:56.960
Awesome.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:57.540
Thank you.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.510
I'm starting to see a lot of the
questions coming in the chat.

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.960
And thanks so much to the
Posit team for helping me

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:04.557
organize these.

00:10:04.557 --> 00:10:06.140
But Libby, I see you
asked a question.

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.740
Do you want to jump in?

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:09.240
Yeah.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.220
I was just wondering
if we could get

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.040
an overview of some
of the big projects

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.720
that you've been
working on at Novartis.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.180
I know that Novartis
is a pharma company

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:19.590
and that it's really big.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:23.160
But being a person outside
of the pharma universe,

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.395
I sometimes don't know what
you guys are working on.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:33.680
You want to start on this one,
Bo, and then I will take next.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.320
Sure.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:38.230
So two of the bigger projects
I've been working on,

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:41.410
one I've been working on
for a couple of years.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:46.340
Basically, when a clinical
trial is being conducted,

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.720
there are a lot of data
analysis on the patient data

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:53.680
that we collect
from the enrollees.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.230
And for later stage--

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:01.140
there are like earlier phases
trials and a small amount

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:02.580
of patients with lower doses.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.040
And then once the
safety established,

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:09.360
you move on to later phases
with larger number of patients

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.780
and higher doses for efficacy
analysis for example.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:15.300
But efficient safety
is always monitored

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.760
through all the phases.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:24.120
And for later stage studies,
the "sponsor"-- in pharma lingo,

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:26.340
the sponsor's the
pharmaceutical company that

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:28.560
pays for the clinical trial--

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:32.100
anyone inside the
sponsor company

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:36.870
are not supposed to know
the actual treatment

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:39.450
of the patients
given if the study is

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:42.210
a randomized controlled trial.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:47.640
So we need to hire
external partners.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.740
Excuse me.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:50.240
Excuse.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.490
Me

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:53.780
Bless you.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:55.400
Thank you.

00:11:55.400 --> 00:12:00.980
We as sponsors have to
hire external experts

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:05.330
to be unblinded to the
actual treatment code

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:07.820
and review safety
data to make sure

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:12.840
that all the patients in the
ongoing trial are still safe.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:15.020
So one of the big
projects that I've

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:16.910
been working on in the
past couple of years

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:23.810
is writing and managing
an app, a Shiny app,

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:28.970
that has a JavaScript backend
to display some of the safety

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:35.480
datas in a more user-friendly
way than say, 200 pages of PDF.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:41.000
Because the DMC members tend
to be very highly regarded.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:43.610
They're expensive-- their
time is very expensive.

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:46.940
Experts in their field, so
they have very limited time

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:50.640
that they can give us
to review the data.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:55.400
So having an app as
a supplementary tool

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.855
aids their review of the
data with the limited time

00:12:58.855 --> 00:12:59.480
that they have.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:03.090
Thank you.

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:04.050
BO WANG: David, to you.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:11.130
Since I joined Novartis, I
have been very lucky regarding

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.530
all the projects I received.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:18.630
Surprisingly, at the beginning
like during my first months,

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:21.820
I was given a project
not about clinical data,

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:24.360
but for human resources data.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:27.870
Because you have to
imagine, Novartis it's

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:32.520
like 100,000 people working and
then you have many departments.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:37.390
It's not only clinical, but it's
also sales, human resources,

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.430
and many other things.

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:42.660
Basically, we had
to develop a tool

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:46.890
to better manage the time
people spend on projects.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:51.240
And this tool was
basically first developed

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:56.220
with Shiny with a lot
of databases around just

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:59.670
to [INAUDIBLE] the
data assistant.

00:13:59.670 --> 00:14:03.960
And then later, the
tool was improved using

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.660
other technologies like React.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:09.510
But in the end, it
shows how you can

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:13.380
integrate Shiny in
the whole ecosystem

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:16.170
and that was a great project.

