00:42:32.590 --> 00:42:35.335
Because I think that's
a novel approach.

00:42:35.335 --> 00:42:36.760
[DOORBELL]

00:42:36.760 --> 00:42:43.240
Someone's getting
their doorbell rung.

00:42:43.240 --> 00:42:43.920
It's Mike.

00:42:47.540 --> 00:42:50.930
So Mike, when you say about
the LEGO pieces are you talking

00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:56.830
about data pipeline
presentation from-- yeah,

00:42:56.830 --> 00:43:02.750
we had some presentation at RN
Pharma some years ago, where,

00:43:02.750 --> 00:43:06.920
yeah, there are some modules
to develop visualization

00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:09.410
for clinical trials.

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:13.310
And then you can combine
those modules together

00:43:13.310 --> 00:43:15.320
to create your
application so you don't

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:19.940
need to develop them yourself.

00:43:19.940 --> 00:43:22.640
And that's basically--

00:43:22.640 --> 00:43:25.550
Yeah, I don't know how
many modules we have,

00:43:25.550 --> 00:43:30.590
but we probably had 30, 32
module, which you can basically

00:43:30.590 --> 00:43:34.580
plug into your app and then
depending what you want to do,

00:43:34.580 --> 00:43:40.640
if you want to do safety
explorer, patient profile,

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:43.400
whatever.

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:48.290
But there is still this issue
where you have application

00:43:48.290 --> 00:43:52.140
with very specific
needs, and in that case,

00:43:52.140 --> 00:43:55.700
people need to dive in the
code and at some point, that's

00:43:55.700 --> 00:43:56.330
inevitable.

00:43:56.330 --> 00:44:04.620
But yeah, for sure that's
a big change for us.

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:09.150
The main challenge is also like
making rules modules, following

00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:14.670
best practices in terms of
software development using renv,

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:18.240
everything with package, CI/CD.

00:44:21.180 --> 00:44:23.230
We are a team, you know?

00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:26.640
We are not even 10 in our team.

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:34.245
So that's a lot on our shoulders
Yeah, I couldn't say it before.

00:44:34.245 --> 00:44:39.990
From time to time, you hear
people are not super satisfied

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:44.700
because this thing is not
doing what it's intended.

00:44:44.700 --> 00:44:46.890
But, in the meantime,
you also have

00:44:46.890 --> 00:44:51.530
people that are really
thankful to what you are doing.

00:44:51.530 --> 00:45:01.590
So that's also rewarding
as a developer.

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:04.340
You can jump on this, Bo.

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:07.280
We have a bunch
of questions so

00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:10.850
if it's OK if I kind of rapid
fire go through a few of them

00:45:10.850 --> 00:45:12.350
here.

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:15.610
Another one on
Slido was my group

00:45:15.610 --> 00:45:19.780
is using bs4Dash and
Shiny pretty extensively

00:45:19.780 --> 00:45:22.150
and I really love it.

00:45:22.150 --> 00:45:25.060
The biggest frustration
that this person runs into

00:45:25.060 --> 00:45:27.940
is if they want to
put in custom CSS.

00:45:27.940 --> 00:45:30.290
And, David, they were wondering
if you have any advice.

00:45:36.020 --> 00:45:38.960
It depends what
they want to achieve,

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:41.990
but if they want to
have questions in CSS

00:45:41.990 --> 00:45:46.310
and bs4Dash, they won't be
able to use [? bsLeap, ?]

00:45:46.310 --> 00:45:51.710
because bs4Dash actually
uses like a refactor

00:45:51.710 --> 00:45:55.470
version of Bootstrap,
specifically Bootstrap 4.

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:57.680
So if you ever
use Bootstrap kit,

00:45:57.680 --> 00:45:59.990
you will have the same
variables, the same size

00:45:59.990 --> 00:46:00.550
variables.

00:46:00.550 --> 00:46:01.430
So it won't work.

00:46:03.990 --> 00:46:07.280
So if you want to develop
something for the long run,

00:46:07.280 --> 00:46:12.500
I would say may want
to go changing Sass--

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:13.110
the Sass code.

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:18.140
So when I say Sass, it's
not SAS as a statistician,

00:46:18.140 --> 00:46:22.070
it's Sass, Syntactically
Awesome Style Sheets.

00:46:22.070 --> 00:46:26.285
But that's another language.

00:46:28.770 --> 00:46:29.270
So yeah.

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:31.080
There is a bit of
learning curve.

00:46:31.080 --> 00:46:35.670
But in the long run, you better
organize your style in the app

00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:38.060
and that's the best
practice to grow.

00:46:38.060 --> 00:46:41.500
If it's just like
10 lines of CSS,

00:46:41.500 --> 00:46:43.520
you may not want to use Sass.

00:46:43.520 --> 00:46:48.040
So it depends how many
they want to customize.

00:46:50.990 --> 00:46:53.150
I look, there's
another question.

00:46:53.150 --> 00:46:54.830
I think it was from Damian.

00:46:54.830 --> 00:46:57.950
If you're still on on
interacting with users,

00:46:57.950 --> 00:47:00.040
do you want to ask that?

00:47:00.040 --> 00:47:00.670
Yeah, sure.

00:47:00.670 --> 00:47:01.220
Hi, everyone.

00:47:01.220 --> 00:47:01.970
My name is Damian.

00:47:01.970 --> 00:47:03.840
I'm the president of Appsilon.

00:47:03.840 --> 00:47:06.430
I'm really happy to be
here and really enjoying

00:47:06.430 --> 00:47:07.930
the conference so far.

00:47:07.930 --> 00:47:09.315
Right now, I'm in Bulgaria.

00:47:09.315 --> 00:47:11.440
But I'm actually on
workcation, so I just came back

00:47:11.440 --> 00:47:13.690
from India and Vietnam.

00:47:13.690 --> 00:47:16.480
So really enjoying
the remote setup.

00:47:16.480 --> 00:47:18.220
I wanted to ask you
about the users.

00:47:18.220 --> 00:47:22.000
Because the beauty of Shiny
is that we can build apps

00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:23.950
like very fast in
small iterations

00:47:23.950 --> 00:47:26.860
and we can keep getting the
feedback and upgrading on them.

00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:30.580
And this makes it like a
bleeding edge kind of software

00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:32.290
that we can provide
to the users.

00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:34.300
And I know that you also
shared that it is not

00:47:34.300 --> 00:47:37.100
that easy to get feedback
from the users directly.

00:47:37.100 --> 00:47:39.070
So I hope to hear a
little bit more about it.

00:47:39.070 --> 00:47:40.315
How do you cope with this?

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:47.890
So just to know whether your
application are properly used,

00:47:47.890 --> 00:47:52.250
whether these kind
of things right

00:47:52.250 --> 00:47:57.230
on how people are using
the applications typically.

00:47:57.230 --> 00:48:00.620
So this is, I'll
be honest, this is

00:48:00.620 --> 00:48:04.980
something we will start
to do at Novartis.

00:48:04.980 --> 00:48:09.080
So I did it like
in the open source,

00:48:09.080 --> 00:48:12.840
but I am really talking what
we are doing at Novartis.

00:48:12.840 --> 00:48:15.266
So what we have?

00:48:15.266 --> 00:48:19.820
We have the tracker in Posit
Connect, which basically-- it's

00:48:19.820 --> 00:48:24.860
an application I developed
last year when I was in Japan.

00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:30.800
So when you travel,
you can do many things.

00:48:30.800 --> 00:48:36.920
And this queries the API
to get all applications.

00:48:36.920 --> 00:48:40.130
For all application,
you get who is accessing

00:48:40.130 --> 00:48:44.240
the application, how much
time, which basically give you

00:48:44.240 --> 00:48:46.430
some insights.

00:48:46.430 --> 00:48:49.820
Like if you develop an
application for several teams,

00:48:49.820 --> 00:48:54.530
you can have some insight
of which team is using

00:48:54.530 --> 00:48:57.140
the most this
application and how

00:48:57.140 --> 00:48:59.360
you could target your supports.

00:48:59.360 --> 00:49:02.000
Like if you see one team
is using the application

00:49:02.000 --> 00:49:05.360
20 times more than
another one, maybe you

00:49:05.360 --> 00:49:11.280
want to invest more people
to provide two paths here.

00:49:11.280 --> 00:49:14.510
And so this is like
a global solution

00:49:14.510 --> 00:49:18.080
that you can apply to all
applications that are deployed.

00:49:18.080 --> 00:49:21.590
And that's not something you
need to add to the application.

00:49:21.590 --> 00:49:23.930
And that that's
important that that's

00:49:23.930 --> 00:49:25.850
something that works
automatically as soon

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:29.000
as the app is on the server.

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:32.270
And, on the other side, if you
want to know how people are

00:49:32.270 --> 00:49:37.500
using the app,
like in-app usage,

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:41.030
you may know like ShinyHeatmap--

00:49:41.030 --> 00:49:43.790
that's a study
from an experiment

00:49:43.790 --> 00:49:48.860
I made around heatmap.js,
a JavaScript library.

00:49:48.860 --> 00:49:51.740
Because in the past I
was using [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:51.740 --> 00:49:54.710
to track usage on my website.

00:49:54.710 --> 00:49:59.870
And one day, I was just
wondering why shouldn't I

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:01.170
just use ShinyHeatmap?

00:50:01.170 --> 00:50:05.510
Oh, sorry, I would use
[INAUDIBLE] on my Shiny apps.

00:50:05.510 --> 00:50:09.470
So I tried the tracking code
to put it in the Shiny app

00:50:09.470 --> 00:50:11.900
and then I couldn't
see anything.

00:50:11.900 --> 00:50:12.840
It didn't work.

00:50:12.840 --> 00:50:16.580
And I collected this report
and they told me, yes, it

00:50:16.580 --> 00:50:18.710
won't work with
Shiny because that's

00:50:18.710 --> 00:50:21.980
quite specific technology.

00:50:21.980 --> 00:50:26.870
And then I took the heatmap.js
and brought some very simple

00:50:26.870 --> 00:50:30.800
code with R and JavaScript,
put it in a package

00:50:30.800 --> 00:50:34.010
and released the ShinyHeatmap.

00:50:34.010 --> 00:50:36.230
So basically, each time
you click in your app,

00:50:36.230 --> 00:50:39.800
it will record the
x and y location,

00:50:39.800 --> 00:50:44.130
and then aggregate the
data at the end of the day.

00:50:44.130 --> 00:50:47.570
So you can see where
people are clicking.

00:50:47.570 --> 00:50:50.120
For instance, you can
identify some dead zones

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:55.070
in the application which
will help you to refactor

00:50:55.070 --> 00:50:56.890
the design at some point.

00:50:56.890 --> 00:51:00.500
If you realize there is
an app that nobody visits,

00:51:00.500 --> 00:51:03.170
maybe that's because
it's badly designed

00:51:03.170 --> 00:51:07.010
or because, for
whatever reason, yeah.

00:51:07.010 --> 00:51:12.320
So that's essentially what
we would like to bring.

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:17.900
And there is also user feedback
like sending communications

00:51:17.900 --> 00:51:20.270
to users.

00:51:20.270 --> 00:51:24.110
So this one is a bit
more active and you

00:51:24.110 --> 00:51:26.600
can be frustrated,
because sometimes you

00:51:26.600 --> 00:51:28.340
don't get the answer.

00:51:28.340 --> 00:51:29.720
People are very busy.

00:51:29.720 --> 00:51:34.700
And then-- Yeah, that's it.

00:51:34.700 --> 00:51:35.880
Thank you.

00:51:35.880 --> 00:51:40.530
I know we are quickly coming
to the end of our hour here

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:42.420
unfortunately.

00:51:42.420 --> 00:51:46.410
So our Posit team is
talking behind the scenes

00:51:46.410 --> 00:51:48.600
that there's so many
awesome questions here

00:51:48.600 --> 00:51:51.900
that we will think about how we
can gather any questions that

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:54.700
didn't get answered
and maybe think

00:51:54.700 --> 00:51:56.685
about a blog post or
some other resource.

00:51:56.685 --> 00:51:58.060
So I just wanted
to let everybody

00:51:58.060 --> 00:52:01.060
know that in case your
questions didn't get answered,

00:52:01.060 --> 00:52:03.880
there's so many great ones.

00:52:03.880 --> 00:52:06.820
Niels, I see you had asked
something in the chat.

00:52:06.820 --> 00:52:10.360
I think around using APIs
in addition to Shiny.

00:52:10.360 --> 00:52:13.730
Do you want to jump
in and ask that?

00:52:13.730 --> 00:52:16.730
NIELS: Yes, sure.

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:20.360
So I was wondering, especially
for bigger applications,

00:52:20.360 --> 00:52:26.090
if you want to separate server
from cloud side rendering,

00:52:26.090 --> 00:52:30.800
maybe add using
React as a front-end

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:33.410
and then separate it from the
back end using [? firmer ?]

00:52:33.410 --> 00:52:34.760
APIs might be easier.

00:52:34.760 --> 00:52:38.165
Is that something you would do
as well or only pure you Shiny?

00:52:42.940 --> 00:52:46.570
From app supporting
clinical projects

00:52:46.570 --> 00:52:51.010
is an easier answer is
that we don't use anything

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:52.780
beside Shiny right now.

00:52:52.780 --> 00:52:55.000
Because first, we
don't really have

00:52:55.000 --> 00:53:01.510
super big app, super complicated
app, and super big sample size.

00:53:01.510 --> 00:53:03.340
Clinical trial data
are quite small

00:53:03.340 --> 00:53:05.980
compared to many
other industries.

00:53:05.980 --> 00:53:08.440
And second, is we
actually tried React

00:53:08.440 --> 00:53:11.650
before separating the
front end and back

00:53:11.650 --> 00:53:15.160
end just to improve flexibility
and the visual effect

00:53:15.160 --> 00:53:16.570
to make it look better.

00:53:16.570 --> 00:53:19.330
But eventually, like
I said in the comment,

00:53:19.330 --> 00:53:21.490
our team is very small.

00:53:21.490 --> 00:53:26.380
Like David mentioned, our
goal is not to write a tool,

00:53:26.380 --> 00:53:29.020
maintain a tool for each
study that come to us,

00:53:29.020 --> 00:53:31.180
but to create something
that we can ship off

00:53:31.180 --> 00:53:34.720
to our statisticians
to use on their own

00:53:34.720 --> 00:53:36.040
and to manage on their own.

00:53:36.040 --> 00:53:39.640
And we have much, much
better adoption of R

00:53:39.640 --> 00:53:44.320
especially with the younger
generation of statisticians

00:53:44.320 --> 00:53:47.620
coming in from academia.

00:53:47.620 --> 00:53:52.180
Many of them are
quite fluent in R,

00:53:52.180 --> 00:53:57.040
but I think we haven't
made the pivot.

00:53:57.040 --> 00:54:00.400
Well, I don't think
it's necessary for trial

00:54:00.400 --> 00:54:01.370
statisticians.

00:54:01.370 --> 00:54:06.710
R is still the one
of the best languages

00:54:06.710 --> 00:54:13.670
that they are the most familiar
with coming in from academia.

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:15.770
For some other bigger
scale apps like the one

00:54:15.770 --> 00:54:19.565
that David worked on for the
HR data, or the POS, maybe.

00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:24.450
You're right, Bo.

00:54:24.450 --> 00:54:27.000
I totally agree.

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:30.270
We decided to go for
R. At least tomorrow,

00:54:30.270 --> 00:54:34.470
we say, people, yeah, we have to
go to [? Julia ?] or whatever.

00:54:34.470 --> 00:54:36.990
At some point,
there'll be a big plus.

00:54:36.990 --> 00:54:38.490
R is doing what we want to do.

00:54:38.490 --> 00:54:40.380
It's doing it well.

00:54:40.380 --> 00:54:41.790
Now we have many tools.

00:54:41.790 --> 00:54:43.350
If we want to speed
up Shiny apps,

00:54:43.350 --> 00:54:49.080
we use parallel packages to
some speed up computations.

00:54:49.080 --> 00:54:52.140
If we start to develop
something with React,

00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:56.910
the problem is we are like
three in the team to React.

00:54:56.910 --> 00:54:59.640
So if we leave in
the future, then

00:54:59.640 --> 00:55:03.330
who is going to
maintain the tool?

00:55:03.330 --> 00:55:07.550
So if we develop it in R,
maybe it's not as fast.

00:55:07.550 --> 00:55:09.590
Maybe it's not as optimized.

00:55:09.590 --> 00:55:14.120
But at least we can find someone
to maintain it in the future.

00:55:14.120 --> 00:55:16.400
And this is what
matters when you are

00:55:16.400 --> 00:55:18.080
in the industry at some point.

